\section*{Methods}

\subsection*{Plasmid Loading and Sequence Retrieval}
Annotated vector sequences for $\text{pcDNA4/TO}$ (target expression vector) and $\text{pAcGFP1-C1}$ (fluorescent tagging vector) were imported from the SnapGene resource library \cite{snapgene}. The donor template containing the $\text{Rab5}$ gene ($\text{mRFP-Rab5}$) was obtained from Addgene and opened in SnapGene to review open reading frames (ORFs) and structural features \cite{addgene}.


\subsection*{Primer Design and In Silico PCR Simulation}
Custom primers were designed to amplify the $\text{Rab5}$ gene from the template. The forward primer incorporated at a $\text{BspEI}$ restriction site at the $5^{\prime}$ end starting at the initial leucine position to maintain proper reading frame alignment with $\text{AcGFP1}$ \cite{vib}. The reverse primer was mapped adjacent to the stop codon utilizing a downstream restriction site $\text{BamHI}$ \cite{vib}. Primer melting temperatures ($T_m$) were calculated within the $58.4\text{--}68.9^{\circ}\text{C}$ range using SnapGene's built-in thermodynamic calculator \cite{vib}. Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and agarose gel electrophoresis were simulated, successfully verifying the expected 711 bp product.


\subsection*{Restriction Cloning and Recombinant Assembly}
Restriction digests were simulated on the amplified $\text{Rab5}$ fragment and on both vector backbones using $\text{BspEI}$ and $\text{BamHI}$ restriction enzymes \cite{vib}. In parallel, the $\text{Rab5}$ insert was ligated into the multiple cloning site (MCS) of $\text{pAcGFP1-C1}$ to generate the $\text{AcGFP-Rab5}$ fusion construct, and independently into the MCS of $\text{pcDNA4/TO}$ to generate the untagged $\text{Rab5}$ construct, each under the control of its respective CMV promoter \cite{vib}. These two constructs represent separate cloning outcomes rather than sequential steps of a single pathway. Plasmid construction pathways were validated via SnapGene's built-in history tracking tool \cite{vib}.


