\subsection*{Bacterial Cell Preparation and Induction}
Competent E. coli DH5 alpha cells were transformed with the recombinant plasmids $\text{pGLO}$ The plasmids were heat shocked at 42 C° for 45 seconds and held in SOC medium at 37 C° for a one hour recovery. The  three lineages were plated on lysogeny broth agar plates under three conditions. 1. Non selective control (-Arabinose/+Antibiotic), 2. Uninduced selective control (-Arabinose/+Antibiotic), 3. Induced selective plate (+Arabinose/+Antibiotic). Plates were incubated for 16 hours at 37 C°. Colony growth and GFP expression was observed using UV light (Figure 2A). Single GFP-expressing colonies were selected and grown in liquid culture to obtain sufficient biomass for DNA extraction.

\subsection*{Plasmid DNA Isolation}
To obtain DNA from transformed colonies PureLink TM  HiPure Plasmid DNA Purification Kits midiprep procedure was followed.\cite{Plasmid DNA MiniPrep Kit}

\subsection*{DNA Quantification}
Plasmid DNA harvest with miniprep of pGLO plasmid was quantified by the course instructor using a NanoDrop microvolume spectrophotometer. Absorbances were measured at 230 nm, 260 nm, 280 nm, and 360 nm. The resulting DNA concentration (ng/µL), A260/230, and A260/280 purity ratios were provided to the class for analysis. 

\subsection*{Gel Electrophoresis}
The extracted DNA was observed by conducting a gel electrophoresis using a 1\%  agarose gel. The DNA within the gel was visualized using BioRad's UView.\cite{loading dye}

\section*{Results}

\subsection*{Initial Plasmid Mapping and Vector Verification}

In silico screening successfully mapped and verified the baseline configurations of the working vectors and templates (Figure~\ref{fig:diseno}). Analysis of the empty $\text{pcDNA4/TO}$ expression vector (5078 bp) confirmed the presence of the CMV promoter, tet operators, multiple cloning site (MCS), and resistance markers (Figure~\ref{fig:diseno}A). The $\text{pAcGFP1-C1}$ reporter plasmid (4731 bp) was mapped, establishing the baseline framework carrying the $\text{AcGFP1}$ gene under CMV control (Figure~\ref{fig:diseno}B). Additionally, the mRFP-Rab5 donor plasmid (5319 bp) was successfully loaded from Addgene to serve as the template source for the $\text{Rab5}$ gene (Figure~\ref{fig:diseno}C).


\subsection*{In Silico PCR Amplification and Primer Binding}

Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) simulation successfully amplified the target $\text{Rab5}$ sequence, yielding the expected 711 bp linear amplicon flanked by the designed restriction sites (Figure~\ref{fig:diseno}D). Sequence inspection verified that the forward primer ($\text{PrimerRab5fw}$) correctly incorporated the $\text{BspEI}$ restriction site at the $5^{\prime}$ start site while maintaining the correct reading frame for the N-terminus (Figure~\ref{fig:diseno}E). Similarly, the reverse primer ($\text{PrimerRab5rev}$) successfully incorporated the downstream $\text{BamHI}$ site adjacent to the stop codon for C-terminal alignment (Figure~\ref{fig:diseno}F).


\subsection*{Recombinant Construct Assembly and Verification}

Restriction cloning workflows successfully generated and validated the final expression constructs (Figure~\ref{fig:diseno}). Insertion of the untagged $\text{Rab5}$ gene into the MCS of $\text{pcDNA4/TO}$ yielded the final $\text{pcDNA4TO-Rab5}$ recombinant plasmid (5733 bp) (Figure~\ref{fig:diseno}G). Concurrently, restriction digest and ligation of the $\text{Rab5}$ fragment with $\text{pAcGFP1-C1}$ produced the $\text{acGFP-Rab5}$ fusion construct (5367 bp) driven by the CMV promoter (Figure~\ref{fig:diseno}H). Both final constructs were independently generated and validated using SnapGene's history tracking tool.

\subsection*{Bacterial Growth and GFP Induction}

Following the transformation and plating of E.coli cultures with the pGLO plasmid on various media, bacterial growth and fluorescence were evaluated to confirm plasmid uptake and gene regulation. On the non-selective control plate (LB/-pGLO), a dense lawn of bacterial growth was observed under UV light, confirming the viability of the starting cultures (Figure 2A). Antibiotic effectiveness was confirmed by an unpictured selective negative control (LB+Amp/-pGLO) that was evaluated to have no bacteria growth, confirming that ampicillin was active and successfully killed all untransformed E.coli. Additionally, in a unpictured LB agar supplemented with ampicillin and arabinose (LB+Amp+Ara), transformed cells demonstrated dense colony growth among the streak lines and intense green fluorescence under UV illumination. This phenotype confirms both successful plasmid uptake (conferring ampicillin resistance via the bla gene) and arabinose-induced expression of green fluorescent protein under the pBAD promoter.
On the uninducible selective control plates (LB+Amp/-Arabinose/+pGLO), bacterial colonies grew successfully confirming that the cells that incorporated the pGLO plasmid and expressed the ampicillin resistance gene. However, these colonies under UV light did not fluoresce because the pBAD promoter remained inactive in the absence of arabinose (Figure 2B). On the induced selective plate containing both ampicillin and arabinose (LB+Amp+Arabinose/+pGLO), the resulting colonies both grew and fluoresced brightly under UV light. (Figure 2C). This confirmed the successful arabinose-mediated activation of the pBAD promoter and subsequent expression of GFP.

\subsection*{Plasmid DNA Extraction and Spectrophotometric Analysis}
Plasmid DNA concentrations across all analyzed samples were below the standard lower detection threshold (2.0 ng/µL) for microvolume spectrophotometers, ranging from 0.68 ng/µL to 2.73 ng/µL (Figure 2D). The lower limit of reliable quantification for dsDNA on standard Nanodrop instruments is typically around 2.0 ng/µL \cite{nanodrop-reference}. For chemical and salt purity, the target A260/A230 range is 2.0-2.2.  All samples displayed low A260/A230 ratios between 0.29-0.93, with a class median of 0.48 (Figure 2E). These low ratio values indicate background UV absorption at 230nm, reflecting co-purified salts or organic solvent from the extraction buffers. The target value of protein purity (A280/A260) is 1.8 for pure dsDNA (Figure 2F). Sample 1 (1.79) and sample 5 (1.89) aligned closely with the ideal DNA purity. Elevated rations were observed in samples 2 (2.25) and sample 3 (2.17), whereas sample 6 exhibits a low ratio of 1.17, indicative of residual protein or other possible UV-absorbing contaminants.

\subsection*{Agarose Gel Electrophoresis Verification}
 No DNA extracted in the protocol was detected in the agarose gel. This might be attributable to human error or poor centripetal force applied due to equipment constraints. 

\subsection*{Plasmid Fitness Analysis}
For the agarose gel picture processed in ImageJ.JS \cite{imageJ}, a model of fitness for the two employed ladders was obtained. For 1kb ladder, bands interpreted were those between 250 pb and 3000 pb due to resolution of image (Figure 2G). For these chain weights the model that best describes migrated distance ($x$) against molecular weight (MW) with an $R^2=0.977$ was the polynomial model:
\[
\text{MW}=0.0879x^2-62.5 x +11410 \quad (\text{Figure 2H)}
\]
For the 100~bp ladder, the model that best describes the relationship between migrated distance and molecular weight with an $R^2 = 0.987$ was also polynomial:
\[
\text{MW} = 0.0529 x^2 - 42.7 x + 8546 \quad (\text{Figure 2I})
\]




